/-!
# Milne, Algebraic Number Theory, Chapter 4: results in Mathlib

Citation file for J. S. Milne, *Algebraic Number Theory*, version 3.08 (2020), bibliography key
`milne2020ant`, Chapter 4 (the finiteness of the class number). It covers the items of the chapter that
are already in Mathlib.

The convention is as follows.

* When one Mathlib declaration states the book's result in essentially the book's form, the declaration
  is tagged directly with `attribute [kb "milne2020ant" "..."]`. A plain comment above the attribute names
  the item and any difference in form (for example a special case, or one direction of an equivalence).
* When the book's form differs from Mathlib's (several Mathlib lemmas combined, different packaging, an
  equivalence against one direction, a general statement against the number field case), this file proves a
  short restating theorem in the book's form from the Mathlib results. The docstring says in words what is
  claimed, which Mathlib declarations are restated, and what differs from the book.
* Locators have the form `v3.08, <kind> <number>, p. <printed page>`. The printed page is the pdf page minus 2.

Items of Chapter 4 that are not in Mathlib (Examples 4.6 to 4.8, Corollary 4.10, Remark 4.11, Lemma 4.23)
are not cited here; Theorem 4.29 is formalized in QuadFormsKB and Remarks 4.12 and 4.28 and Aside 4.30
state no result.
-/

open MeasureTheory NumberField NumberField.mixedEmbedding
open scoped Classical

namespace NumberFieldKB.MilneANT

-- Milne, Proposition 4.1 (a): the norm of the extension of an ideal is its power by the degree.
-- Mathlib states it for a finite torsion-free extension of Dedekind domains, with the exponent the rank of
-- the extension as a module (equal to [L : K] when the extension of rings is free).
attribute [kb "milne2020ant" "v3.08, Prop. 4.1 (a), p. 69 (exponent is the module rank)"]
  Ideal.relNorm_algebraMap

-- Milne, Proposition 4.1 (b): in the Galois case the norm of a prime P over p is a power of p.
-- Weaker form only: Mathlib proves that the relative norm of P is p to the power of the inertia degree.
-- The product over the Galois conjugates of P is not stated.
attribute [kb "milne2020ant" "v3.08, Prop. 4.1 (b), p. 69 (weaker form: N P = p^f only)"]
  Ideal.relNorm_eq_pow_of_isPrime_isGalois

-- Milne, Proposition 4.1 (c): the norm of the principal ideal generated by an element is the principal
-- ideal generated by its norm. Same statement for Dedekind domains in general.
attribute [kb "milne2020ant" "v3.08, Prop. 4.1 (c), p. 69"] Ideal.relNorm_singleton

-- Milne, Proposition 4.2 (a): the ideal norm of an ideal of the ring of integers is the principal ideal
-- generated by the index. Same statement: the relative norm to the integers is generated by `absNorm`.
-- Multiplicativity of the numerical norm is the fact that `Ideal.absNorm` is a monoid homomorphism with zero.
attribute [kb "milne2020ant" "v3.08, Prop. 4.2 (a), p. 69"] Ideal.relNorm_int

-- Milne, Proposition 4.2 (a) and the definition of the numerical norm before it: the numerical norm of an
-- ideal is the index of the ideal in the ring (here the cardinality of the quotient).
attribute [kb "milne2020ant" "v3.08, Prop. 4.2 (a), p. 69 (numerical norm is the index)"]
  Ideal.absNorm_apply

-- Milne, Proposition 4.2 (b): the index of fractional ideals. Only part of the statement is in Mathlib:
-- the absolute norm of fractional ideals as a multiplicative map to the rational numbers; the formula
-- for the index (a : b) as the norm of a^{-1} b is not stated, so this declaration is cited as the
-- definition used for it, not as the formula.
attribute [kb "milne2020ant" "v3.08, Prop. 4.2 (b), p. 70 (the norm of fractional ideals; formula not stated)"]
  FractionalIdeal.absNorm

-- Milne, Theorem 4.3 (every ideal class contains an integral ideal of norm at most the Minkowski bound).
-- Same statement: the bound is (4/pi)^s * (n!/n^n) * sqrt(|disc|), with s the number of complex places.
attribute [kb "milne2020ant" "v3.08, Thm. 4.3, p. 70"] NumberField.exists_ideal_in_class_of_norm_le

-- Milne, Theorem 4.4 (the class number is finite). Mathlib's instance says the class group of the ring
-- of integers is a finite type; `NumberField.classNumber` is its cardinality.
attribute [kb "milne2020ant" "v3.08, Thm. 4.4, p. 70"] NumberField.RingOfIntegers.instFintypeClassGroup

/-- Milne, Example 4.5: the ring of Gaussian integers $\mathbb Z[i]$ is a principal ideal domain.

This restates the Euclidean domain structure `GaussianInt.instEuclideanDomain`: a Euclidean domain is a
principal ideal domain. The book proves this from the Minkowski bound for $\mathbb Q[i]$ and then remarks
that the elementary proof shows more, namely that the ring is Euclidean; Mathlib proves the Euclidean
property directly, not via the Minkowski bound. -/
@[kb "milne2020ant" "v3.08, Example 4.5, p. 71"]
theorem ex_4_5 : IsPrincipalIdealRing GaussianInt := inferInstance

/-- Milne, Theorem 4.9: there is no unramified extension of $\mathbb Q$ other than $\mathbb Q$.

A finite extension $K$ of $\mathbb Q$ in which no prime number ramifies (that is, the prime ideal generated
by each prime number $p$ is unramified in the integral closure of the integers in $K$) has degree one. This
restates `NumberField.exists_not_isUnramifiedIn` (a ramified prime exists when the degree is not one),
which Mathlib proves from the Hermite-Minkowski bound `NumberField.abs_discr_gt_two` as in the book. -/
@[kb "milne2020ant" "v3.08, Thm. 4.9, p. 72"]
theorem thm_4_9 (K 𝒪 : Type*) [Field K] [NumberField K] [CommRing 𝒪] [Algebra 𝒪 K]
    [IsIntegralClosure 𝒪 ℤ K]
    (h : ∀ p : ℕ, p.Prime → Algebra.IsUnramifiedIn 𝒪 (Ideal.span {(p : ℤ)})) :
    Module.finrank ℚ K = 1 := by
  by_contra hne
  obtain ⟨p, hp, hnot⟩ := NumberField.exists_not_isUnramifiedIn (K := K) (𝒪 := 𝒪) hne
  exact hnot (h p hp)

/-- Milne, Lemma 4.14: for a subgroup $\Lambda$ of a finite-dimensional real vector space, the following
are equivalent: (1) $\Lambda$ is discrete in the induced topology; (2) some open set meets $\Lambda$ only in
$0$; (3) every compact set meets $\Lambda$ in a finite set; (4) every bounded set meets $\Lambda$ in a
finite set.

The statement is given for a subgroup of a finite-dimensional real normed space (the topology is the one
from any norm). This is proved from `Metric.finite_isBounded_inter_isClosed` (implication (1) to (4)),
`AddSubgroup.isClosed_of_discreteTopology` (a discrete subgroup is closed) and
`discreteTopology_iff_isOpen_singleton_zero`; Mathlib does not state the four-way equivalence. -/
@[kb "milne2020ant" "v3.08, Lemma 4.14, p. 73"]
theorem lemma_4_14 {E : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup E] [NormedSpace ℝ E] [FiniteDimensional ℝ E]
    (L : AddSubgroup E) :
    List.TFAE
      [DiscreteTopology L,
       ∃ U : Set E, IsOpen U ∧ U ∩ (L : Set E) = {0},
       ∀ K : Set E, IsCompact K → (K ∩ (L : Set E)).Finite,
       ∀ S : Set E, Bornology.IsBounded S → (S ∩ (L : Set E)).Finite] := by
  tfae_have 1 → 4 := by
    intro h S hS
    have hd : IsDiscrete (L : Set E) := isDiscrete_iff_discreteTopology.2 h
    have hc : IsClosed (L : Set E) := AddSubgroup.isClosed_of_discreteTopology
    exact Metric.finite_isBounded_inter_isClosed hd hS hc
  tfae_have 4 → 3 := fun h K hK => h K hK.isBounded
  tfae_have 3 → 2 := by
    intro h
    have hT : ((Metric.closedBall (0 : E) 1 ∩ (L : Set E)) \ {0}).Finite :=
      (h _ (isCompact_closedBall 0 1)).sdiff
    refine ⟨Metric.ball 0 1 \ ((Metric.closedBall (0 : E) 1 ∩ (L : Set E)) \ {0}),
      Metric.isOpen_ball.sdiff hT.isClosed, ?_⟩
    ext x
    simp only [Set.mem_inter_iff, Set.mem_sdiff, Metric.mem_ball, Metric.mem_closedBall,
      Set.mem_singleton_iff, SetLike.mem_coe, dist_zero_right, not_and, not_not]
    constructor
    · rintro ⟨⟨h1, h2⟩, h3⟩
      exact h2 ⟨h1.le, h3⟩
    · rintro rfl
      simp
  tfae_have 2 → 1 := by
    rintro ⟨U, hU, hUL⟩
    rw [discreteTopology_iff_isOpen_singleton_zero]
    have : ({0} : Set L) = Subtype.val ⁻¹' U := by
      ext x
      constructor
      · rintro rfl
        have : (0 : E) ∈ U ∩ (L : Set E) := by rw [hUL]; rfl
        exact this.1
      · intro hx
        have : (x : E) ∈ U ∩ (L : Set E) := ⟨hx, x.2⟩
        rw [hUL] at this
        exact Subtype.ext this
    rw [this]
    exact hU.preimage continuous_subtype_val
  tfae_finish

/-- Milne, Proposition 4.15: a subgroup of a finite-dimensional real vector space is a lattice if and only
if it is discrete.

Here a lattice is a subgroup generated over the integers by finitely many elements that are linearly
independent over the real numbers (the empty family gives the zero lattice), as in the book. The
subgroup is given as a `ℤ`-submodule. The forward direction extends the independent family to a real basis
and uses the discreteness of the integer span of a basis (`ZSpan` instances). The reverse direction uses
that a discrete submodule is a free finitely generated `ℤ`-module (`instModuleFree_of_discrete_submodule`,
`instModuleFinite_of_discrete_submodule`) and `Real.finrank_eq_int_finrank_of_discrete`, which shows that a
`ℤ`-basis stays linearly independent over the real numbers. -/
@[kb "milne2020ant" "v3.08, Prop. 4.15, p. 74"]
theorem prop_4_15 {E : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup E] [NormedSpace ℝ E] [FiniteDimensional ℝ E]
    (L : Submodule ℤ E) :
    (∃ (r : ℕ) (e : Fin r → E), LinearIndependent ℝ e ∧ L = Submodule.span ℤ (Set.range e)) ↔
      DiscreteTopology L := by
  constructor
  · rintro ⟨r, e, he, rfl⟩
    classical
    have hs := he.linearIndepOn_id
    let b := Module.Basis.extend hs
    have : Finite (hs.extend (Set.subset_univ _)) := Module.Finite.finite_basis b
    have hsub : Submodule.span ℤ (Set.range e) ≤ Submodule.span ℤ (Set.range b) := by
      apply Submodule.span_mono
      rw [show Set.range b = _ from Module.Basis.range_extend hs]
      exact hs.subset_extend _
    have : DiscreteTopology (Submodule.span ℤ (Set.range b)) := inferInstance
    exact DiscreteTopology.of_continuous_injective (continuous_inclusion hsub)
      (Submodule.inclusion_injective hsub)
  · intro hL
    classical
    let b := Module.Free.chooseBasis ℤ L
    let f : Module.Free.ChooseBasisIndex ℤ L → E := fun i => (b i : E)
    have hind : LinearIndependent ℤ f :=
      b.linearIndependent.map' L.subtype (Submodule.ker_subtype L)
    have hspan : Submodule.span ℤ (Set.range f) = L := by
      have : Set.range f = L.subtype '' Set.range b := by
        rw [← Set.range_comp]; rfl
      rw [this, ← Submodule.map_span, b.span_eq, Submodule.map_top, Submodule.range_subtype]
    have hdisc : DiscreteTopology (Submodule.span ℤ (Set.range f)) := by rw [hspan]; exact hL
    have hR : LinearIndependent ℝ f := by
      rw [linearIndependent_iff_card_eq_finrank_span]
      have h1 := Real.finrank_eq_int_finrank_of_discrete hdisc
      have h2 := (linearIndependent_iff_card_eq_finrank_span.1 hind)
      rw [h2]
      exact h1.symm
    refine ⟨Fintype.card (Module.Free.ChooseBasisIndex ℤ L),
      f ∘ (Fintype.equivFin (Module.Free.ChooseBasisIndex ℤ L)).symm, ?_, ?_⟩
    · exact hR.comp _ (Equiv.injective _)
    · rw [Set.range_comp, Equiv.range_eq_univ, Set.image_univ, hspan]

-- Milne, Remark 4.16 (a): the volume of a fundamental parallelopiped of a full lattice in the real
-- coordinate space is the absolute value of the determinant of the basis. Mathlib states it for the
-- fundamental domain of a real basis (the parallelopiped at the origin) of `ι → ℝ`.
attribute [kb "milne2020ant" "v3.08, Remark 4.16 (a), p. 75"] ZSpan.volume_fundamentalDomain

-- Milne, Remark 4.16 (a), independence of the basis: the covolume of a full lattice equals the absolute
-- value of the determinant of any integer basis.
attribute [kb "milne2020ant" "v3.08, Remark 4.16 (a), p. 75 (independence of the choice of basis)"]
  ZLattice.covolume_eq_det

-- Milne, Remark 4.16 (b): for full lattices, the ratio of the covolumes is the index.
attribute [kb "milne2020ant" "v3.08, Remark 4.16 (b), p. 75"]
  ZLattice.covolume_div_covolume_eq_relIndex

/-- Milne, Theorem 4.17 (Blichfeldt): let $\Lambda$ be a full lattice in a finite-dimensional real vector
space $V$ with a fundamental parallelopiped $D_0$, and let $S$ be a measurable subset of $V$. If the
measure of $S$ exceeds that of $D_0$, then $S$ contains distinct points $\alpha$ and $\beta$ with
$\beta - \alpha \in \Lambda$.

This restates `MeasureTheory.exists_pair_mem_lattice_not_disjoint_vadd`, which says two distinct lattice
translates of $S$ meet. Here the lattice is a countable subgroup with a fundamental domain `F` for the
Haar measure (a full lattice has one by `ZLattice.isAddFundamentalDomain`), and `S` is only required
to be null measurable. -/
@[kb "milne2020ant" "v3.08, Thm. 4.17, p. 75"]
theorem thm_4_17 {E : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup E] [NormedSpace ℝ E] [MeasurableSpace E]
    [BorelSpace E] [FiniteDimensional ℝ E] {μ : Measure E} [μ.IsAddHaarMeasure]
    {L : AddSubgroup E} [Countable L] {F S : Set E} (hF : IsAddFundamentalDomain L F μ)
    (hS : NullMeasurableSet S μ) (hvol : μ F < μ S) :
    ∃ α ∈ S, ∃ β ∈ S, α ≠ β ∧ β - α ∈ L := by
  obtain ⟨x, y, hxy, hnd⟩ := exists_pair_mem_lattice_not_disjoint_vadd hF hS hvol
  rw [Set.not_disjoint_iff] at hnd
  obtain ⟨z, ⟨a, ha, rfl⟩, ⟨b, hb, hab⟩⟩ := hnd
  have hab' : (y : E) + b = (x : E) + a := hab
  refine ⟨a, ha, b, hb, ?_, ?_⟩
  · rintro rfl
    apply hxy
    exact Subtype.ext (add_right_cancel hab').symm
  · have : b - a = (x : E) - (y : E) := by
      rw [sub_eq_sub_iff_add_eq_add, add_comm]; exact hab'
    rw [this]
    exact L.sub_mem x.2 y.2

-- Milne, Remark 4.18: a convex set symmetric in the origin whose volume exceeds 2^n times the volume of a
-- fundamental parallelopiped contains a nonzero lattice point. Same statement (strict inequality), for a
-- countable subgroup with a fundamental domain for Haar measure.
attribute [kb "milne2020ant" "v3.08, Remark 4.18, p. 76"]
  MeasureTheory.exists_ne_zero_mem_lattice_of_measure_mul_two_pow_lt_measure

-- Milne, Theorem 4.19 (Minkowski): a compact convex set symmetric in the origin, with volume at least 2^n
-- times the volume of a fundamental parallelopiped, contains a nonzero point of the lattice.
-- Same hypotheses (the subgroup is discrete and has a fundamental domain for Haar measure).
attribute [kb "milne2020ant" "v3.08, Thm. 4.19, p. 76"]
  MeasureTheory.exists_ne_zero_mem_lattice_of_measure_mul_two_pow_le_measure

-- Milne, Remark 4.20: every positive integer is a sum of four squares. Same result; Mathlib's proof does
-- not use Minkowski's theorem.
attribute [kb "milne2020ant" "v3.08, Remark 4.20, p. 76"] Nat.sum_four_squares

/-- Milne, Lemma 4.22: in $V = \mathbb R^r \times \mathbb C^s$ with the norm
$\lVert x \rVert = \sum \lvert x_i \rvert + 2 \sum \lvert z_j \rvert$, the set of points of norm at most $t$
has volume $2^r (\pi/2)^s t^n / n!$, where $n = r + 2s$ and $t > 0$.

This restates `NumberField.mixedEmbedding.convexBodySum_volume`, where the space is the mixed space of a
number field $K$ (with $r$ real and $s$ complex places, $n = [K : \mathbb Q]$), the set is
`convexBodySum K t`, and the constant is written out from `convexBodySumFactor`. The Lebesgue measure on
the complex factors is the one of the plane, as in the book. -/
@[kb "milne2020ant" "v3.08, Lemma 4.22, p. 78"]
theorem lemma_4_22 (K : Type*) [Field K] [NumberField K] (t : ℝ) (ht : 0 < t) :
    volume (convexBodySum K t) =
      ENNReal.ofReal (2 ^ InfinitePlace.nrRealPlaces K * (Real.pi / 2) ^ InfinitePlace.nrComplexPlaces K *
        t ^ Module.finrank ℚ K / (Module.finrank ℚ K).factorial) := by
  rw [convexBodySum_volume]
  simp only [convexBodySumFactor]
  rw [← ENNReal.ofReal_pow ht.le, ← ENNReal.ofReal_coe_nnreal, ← ENNReal.ofReal_mul (by positivity)]
  congr 1
  push_cast
  rw [NNReal.coe_real_pi]
  ring

/-- Milne, Lemma 4.25 (arithmetic-geometric mean inequality): for positive real numbers
$a_1, \dots, a_n$, $\prod a_i \le (\sum a_i)^n / n^n$.

This restates `Real.geom_mean_le_arith_mean` (the weighted form) with all weights equal to one and raises
both sides to the power $n$. The case $n = 0$ is included and trivial. -/
@[kb "milne2020ant" "v3.08, Lemma 4.25, p. 79"]
theorem lemma_4_25 (n : ℕ) (a : Fin n → ℝ) (ha : ∀ i, 0 < a i) :
    ∏ i, a i ≤ (∑ i, a i) ^ n / (n : ℝ) ^ n := by
  rcases Nat.eq_zero_or_pos n with rfl | hn
  · simp
  have hw := Real.geom_mean_le_arith_mean (Finset.univ : Finset (Fin n)) (fun _ => (1 : ℝ)) a
    (fun _ _ => zero_le_one) (by simpa using hn) (fun i _ => (ha i).le)
  simp only [Real.rpow_one, Finset.sum_const, Finset.card_univ, Fintype.card_fin, nsmul_eq_mul,
    mul_one, one_mul] at hw
  have hnpos : (0 : ℝ) < n := by exact_mod_cast hn
  have hprod : 0 ≤ ∏ i, a i := Finset.prod_nonneg fun i _ => (ha i).le
  have h2 : (∏ i, a i) ≤ ((∑ i, a i) / n) ^ n := by
    have := pow_le_pow_left₀ (Real.rpow_nonneg hprod _) hw n
    rwa [← Real.rpow_natCast, ← Real.rpow_mul hprod, inv_mul_cancel₀ hnpos.ne',
      Real.rpow_one] at this
  rwa [div_pow] at h2

/-- Milne, Proposition 4.26: for a nonzero ideal $\mathfrak a$ of the ring of integers of a number field
$K$, the image of $\mathfrak a$ in $V = \mathbb R^r \times \mathbb C^s$ is a full lattice, and a
fundamental parallelopiped has volume $2^{-s} N\mathfrak a \, \lvert \Delta_K \rvert^{1/2}$.

This restates `NumberField.mixedEmbedding.volume_fundamentalDomain_fractionalIdealLatticeBasis` together
with `NumberField.mixedEmbedding.covolume_integerLattice`, and the instance saying that the image
`idealLattice K I` is a $\mathbb Z$-lattice of the mixed space. It is stated for a nonzero fractional ideal
$I$ (an invertible element of the group of fractional ideals), more generally than the book, with the
norm `FractionalIdeal.absNorm` which agrees with `Ideal.absNorm` on integral ideals
(`FractionalIdeal.coeIdeal_absNorm`). The covolume is the volume of any fundamental parallelopiped. -/
@[kb "milne2020ant" "v3.08, Prop. 4.26, p. 79"]
theorem prop_4_26 (K : Type*) [Field K] [NumberField K]
    (I : (FractionalIdeal (nonZeroDivisors (RingOfIntegers K)) K)ˣ) :
    IsZLattice ℝ (idealLattice K I) ∧
    ZLattice.covolume (idealLattice K I) volume =
      (FractionalIdeal.absNorm (I : FractionalIdeal (nonZeroDivisors (RingOfIntegers K)) K) : ℝ) *
        (2⁻¹ ^ InfinitePlace.nrComplexPlaces K * √|(discr K : ℝ)|) := by
  refine ⟨inferInstance, ?_⟩
  have h := covolume_integerLattice K
  rw [ZLattice.covolume_eq_measure_fundamentalDomain _ _ (fundamentalDomain_integerLattice K)] at h
  rw [ZLattice.covolume_eq_measure_fundamentalDomain _ _ (fundamentalDomain_idealLattice K I),
    measureReal_def, volume_fundamentalDomain_fractionalIdealLatticeBasis, ENNReal.toReal_mul,
    ENNReal.toReal_ofReal (Rat.cast_nonneg.2 (FractionalIdeal.absNorm_nonneg _)), ← measureReal_def, h]

-- Milne, Proposition 4.27: every nonzero ideal contains a nonzero element whose norm is at most the
-- Minkowski bound times the norm of the ideal. Same bound, for invertible fractional ideals (more general
-- than the integral ideals of the book).
attribute [kb "milne2020ant" "v3.08, Prop. 4.27, p. 80"]
  NumberField.exists_ne_zero_mem_ideal_of_norm_le_mul_sqrt_discr

end NumberFieldKB.MilneANT
